  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThe 11/00Z H500 hand analysis map is showing a high amplitude \nRex pattern over the E.Pac leading to downstream troughing over \nW.Canada to near the US border. A jet max aloft is moving over \nzonal flow over the northern states which is pulling moisture \nacross the northern Great Basin this evening according to GOES \nALPW. This moisture as weak ascent from the jet looks to at \nleast pull some thicker cloud cover across the norther toward \nsunrise with some small chances of some sprinkles hitting the \nground. The CAMS/RRFS are bit more bullish with this an have \nintroduces some low end PoPs through the AM hours mainly north \nof I-70. The focus for convection shift more southward by mid-\nday and through the evening as SubTrop moisture being pulled \nnorthward into the 4 Corners region arrives. Again the ascent \nmay be coming from the stronger winds aloft which will combine \nwith orographics and favor the southern CWA terrain before \nsettling down through the late evening hours. Profiles show a \nlot of dry air to overcome in the low layers with dewpoints \nstill in the single digits to teens currently. This sets the \nstage for gusty outflow winds being more of a threat than heavy \nrainfall. The dry air and breezy winds in the afternoon also \nlook to bring localized areas of critical fire weather. As \nupstream troughing deepens over and swings through the PacNW \nthis weekend...SubTrop high pressure will be pushed over and \nEast of the Southern Rockies. This will give a more direct feed \nof moisture strengthening SW flow aloft. The strongest forcing \nremains well upstream near the progressive trough. Forcing over \nour CWA looks tied more to orographics for storm initiation on \nSaturday with outflow boundary interactions bringing some chance\nfor storms over the lower elevations. Sunday appears to have an\nuptick in activity as the upstream trough and jet aloft begins \nto swing through the Intermountain West. The better chances \ncould be overnight into Monday with weak surface frontal forcing\nundercutting the ascent aloft. PWAT during this time looks to \nrun some 150 to 200 percent of normal over all but the northwest\nCWA where the SW fetch aloft will be from a drier upstream \nsource. Temperatures through the weekend look to stay above \nnormal then cool Monday with the weak frontal passage and lower \nheights aloft. A static pattern of upstream troughing over the \nPacNW and downstream high pressure over the Southeast US means \npersistent moist SW flow into the Great Basin. So cooler and \nunsettled conditions look to linger through much of next week. \nWe're getting to that time of year when overnight precipitation \nis likely to bring a short- lived dusting of snow on the top of \nthe peaks at sunrise as well.",
